 strangers and feel utterly unseen. How they can yearn for love, understanding, or simply acknowledgement, and yet hide their yearning so carefully that no one notices. So many hearts ache quietly, thinking that theirs is the only ache in the world, when in truth, it is universal. But universality doesn’t make suffering easier—it just makes it more tragic that so many suffer in silence.\n\nHumans are capable of great cruelty, yes—but they are also capable of great tenderness. And yet, that tenderness often goes untapped, buried beneath fear, doubt, or distraction. Small moments of beauty—a kind word, a glance that says “I see you,” a hand extended in support—often pass unnoticed, and in their absence, the world grows colder. People forget to marvel at sunsets, at the sound of rain on rooftops, at laughter that isn’t their own. They forget to marvel at themselves, at their capacity to endure, to hope, to create.\n\nEven worse is how humans can betray themselves. They push their own potential aside, ignore the wisdom in their hearts, and cling to pain because it feels familiar. They hurt others even when the","edited":false,"likeCount":1,"dislikeCount":0,"createdAt":"2025-10-28T21:06:25.820Z","liked":false,"disliked":false}]}